==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|ang|hēræfter}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|/hɪɹˈæftɚ/|lang=en}}
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|/hɪəˈɹɑːftə/|lang=en}}
* {{hyph|lang=en|here|af|ter}}

===Adverb===
{{en-adv|-}}

# {{lb|en|dated}} In [[time]] to come; in some [[future]] time or [[state]].
#* {{quote-book|year=c. 1606|author={{w|William Shakespeare}}|title={{w|Macbeth}}|chapter={{w|Tomorrow and tomorrow and tomorrow|Act V Scene V}}|text=She should have died '''hereafter'''; There would have been a time for such a word.}}
#* Dryden:
#*: '''Hereafter''' he from war shall come.
# [[from now on|From now on]]

====Synonyms====
